1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:06,871
Their first promise in the new arc was modest and specific: every claim on the map would have a method behind it.

2
00:00:06,871 --> 00:00:15,150
In the library, with a textbook open at his hand, Lin began to write the first page of a study guide.

3
00:00:15,150 --> 00:00:20,084
“A vocabulary list is not knowledge,” Maya said.

4
00:00:20,084 --> 00:00:30,678
“A method is.” Their idea was to keep one question in front of every reader: which claim does this page support?

5
00:00:30,678 --> 00:00:35,442
The class met twice a week in a classroom above the library.

6
00:00:35,442 --> 00:00:47,741
Their instructor, an experienced researcher in sociology, gave them a sample, a specimen of bad analysis, and asked for a comment on each.

7
00:00:47,741 --> 00:00:51,555
“Your writing has improved,” she said.

8
00:00:51,555 --> 00:00:54,230
“Your literacy has not.

9
00:00:54,230 --> 00:01:04,189
Where is the example from your own research?” Her expertise was in reading numbers, and she read theirs quickly.

10
00:01:04,189 --> 00:01:13,872
An expert on the frontier of educational tech visited for an instance of what not to do: a blog that promised fluent reading in a week.

11
00:01:13,872 --> 00:01:19,382
The advanced group laughed; the elementary group memorized it.

12
00:01:19,382 --> 00:01:24,071
“Reading a quiz is not reading,” the instructor said.

13
00:01:24,071 --> 00:01:33,180
“An analytic mind asks what the quiz rewards.” Her comment stayed with them longer than the visit did.

14
00:01:33,180 --> 00:01:44,503
They wrote a basic alphabet book for the language centre, a classical example for the disciplinary board, and a ripe blog post about deliberate practice.

15
00:01:44,503 --> 00:01:50,562
Skilful contributors were rare, and a number of them were already tired.

16
00:01:50,562 --> 00:02:06,344
“In turn,” Lin noted, “each member of the board gets one early draft and one week to answer it.” A young contributor from the change office wrote three entries in an afternoon and then vanished for a month.

17
00:02:06,344 --> 00:02:09,604
The study turned toward analysis.

18
00:02:09,604 --> 00:02:21,522
An academic reviewer asked whether a proficient examinee could do anything a skilled one could not, and a sociology student asked the same question from the other side.

19
00:02:21,522 --> 00:02:26,701
“Every textbook hides a choice,” Lin wrote in her notebook.

20
00:02:26,701 --> 00:02:41,827
“We should show the choice.” The lead question of the whole guide was hers: without it, the guide would be another list, and a list is exactly what the missing students do not need.
